.he "rshd (8)                      10/28/26                     rshd (8)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
rshd
- the remote shell server
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
/bin/rshd [ port ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Rshd listens on a tcp socket and serves the classic rsh request over the
network card (sys/NET.md).  The port defaults to 514; the command-line
port exists for the simulator, which binds a high host port for the
guest.
.sp
Each connection is one request:
.sp
.nf
.in +4
<stderr-port> NUL <luser> NUL <ruser> NUL <command> NUL
.in -4
.fi
.sp
The caller is checked against /etc/hosts.equiv and the remote user's
~/.rhosts (/.rhosts for an account whose home is /), by address: the
card's connected event carries the peer's dotted quad.  Root is never
trusted through hosts.equiv, only through its own .rhosts.  There is no
password and no encryption.
.sp
A request with a command runs it with the connection itself as its
standard input, output and error: the login shell's sh -c starts on the
socket, no pty is spent, and the session child forks it and waits so the
daemon's reaping is one child per request.  This is what rcp (1) uses:
rcp -f sends the named files and rcp -t receives them over that
connection.
.sp
A request with an empty command is an interactive login.  The daemon
puts a login shell on a pty slave and shuttles bytes between the master
and the connection, which gives the shell its terminal, its line
discipline and its job control.  There are two pairs, /dev/ptyp0 with
/dev/ttyp0 and /dev/ptyp1 with /dev/ttyp1; a session claims one by
trying each and the driver refuses a pair another session holds, so a
command never spends one.  If both pairs are held the connection is
closed.
.sp
The daemon forks a child per request.  The parent keeps the listener:
LISTEN returns when the card has a connection parked - its one pending
slot - and ACCEPT hands the child the connected socket while the parent
goes back to listening, so two sessions run at once.  A finished child
sends the parent SIGTERM and is reaped on the next pass; a one-second
alarm bounds the wait for a card with no interrupt line.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/dev/net0	the card's socket clone
.br
/dev/ptyp0, /dev/ttyp0	the first pty pair
.br
/dev/ptyp1, /dev/ttyp1	the second
.br
/etc/hosts.equiv	hosts trusted for ordinary users
.br
~/.rhosts	hosts and users trusted for this account
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
rcp (1), netstat (1), sys/NET.md
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUGS
.in +5
.br
There is no stderr back-channel: the request's first field is ignored
and a command's standard error is the same connection as its output.
The authentication is by address, and the request carries the user
names in clear.
